RULE 1 -- BYTES AT THE EDGES, TEXT IN THE MIDDLE
   forbidden:  data = open(p, 'rb').read(); data.replace(b'a', b'b')
               ...then treating data as text for the next 200 lines
   Decode once, at the boundary. Everything inside works on str, and
   the encode happens at the far edge. A program that carries bytes
   through its middle has to remember an encoding at every step.

RULE 2 -- THE ENCODING COMES FROM THE PROTOCOL, NEVER FROM THE BYTES
   these bytes 63 61 66 c3 a9 are:
      valid utf-8     -> 'café'
      valid latin-1   -> 'cafÃ©'
      valid cp1252    -> 'cafÃ©'
   Three readings, all successful, one intended. Nothing in the file
   says which. So the encoding is a fact about the CONTRACT -- the
   Content-Type header, the database column, the interface spec -- and
   guessing is what you do when the contract failed to say.

RULE 3 -- errors= IS A DECISION
   replace          'caf�'
   ignore           'caf'
   surrogateescape  'caf\udce9'
   Every one of those succeeded and every one of them means something
   different about your data. Choosing by which one stops the traceback
   is choosing at random.

RULE 4 -- NORMALIZE BEFORE COMPARING
   'café' == 'café' -> True
   after NFC      -> True
   forbidden:  if user_input == stored_name:
   ...on anything that came from a filesystem, a browser or a Mac.

RULE 5 -- 'LENGTH' HAS THREE ANSWERS, SO SAY WHICH
   'café'       code points 4   utf-8 bytes 5   utf-16 units 4
   '😀'          code points 1   utf-8 bytes 4   utf-16 units 2
   'é'         code points 2   utf-8 bytes 3   utf-16 units 2
   forbidden:  VARCHAR(20) with no note about what 20 counts
   A field limit, a truncation and a progress bar are three different
   questions, and only one of them is about bytes.

THE RULES AS ONE SENTENCE
   Decode at the door, work in text, encode at the far door, and write
   the encoding down in the contract -- because the bytes will not
   remember it for you and neither will the next person.
